  "notes": "West leads the ♠J against 4♥. South must lose tricks to the ♥A, the ♥K, and the ♦A. The defense has attacked spades and threatens to take the setting trick in that suit. South must play to dispose of his spade loser on dummy's third diamond.\n\nAfter winning the opening lead, South should attack diamonds by leading the ♦K. The defense may duck the first round, but South continues diamonds with the ♦2, knocking out the ♦A.\n\nDummy now has an established diamond winner and the ♣A as an entry to it. The defense will continue spades, but South can win, cross to the ♣A, and discard his spade loser on the diamond winner in dummy. South gives up tricks to the ♥A and ♥K, making his contract.\n\nIt would be a mistake to play trumps before diamonds. The defense would win, and persist in spades. South would now be unable to avoid the loss of a spade trick.",
